WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.503
In 1902 a paleontologist named Barnum Brown found an exciting new fossil.

2
00:00:06.504 --> 00:00:13.828
He found it while he was on a fossil-hunting expedition in Montana, at a site filled with ancient rocks.

3
00:00:13.829 --> 00:00:22.162
Brown was a very well-known and experienced fossil collector who had traveled all over the world hunting for dinosaur fossils.

4
00:00:22.163 --> 00:00:31.314
And he had just discovered the remains of what is now considered the most famous dinosaur that ever lived: the Tyrannosaurus rex

5
00:00:31.315 --> 00:00:34.485
. The new fossil was located in solid

6
00:00:34.486 --> 00:00:42.317
rock. In the early days of paleontology, the methods for excavating, or digging out, a fossil were not very sophisticated.

7
00:00:42.318 --> 00:00:46.899
So Brown had to use dynamite to blast the rock into pieces.

8
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:55.697
Then these broken rock fragments, which weighed hundreds of kilograms, were shipped to the Museum of Natural History in New York City.

9
00:00:55.698 --> 00:01:02.171
At that time the Museum of Natural History had one of the best dinosaur fossil collections in the world.

10
00:01:02.172 --> 00:01:09.729
Henry Osborn, a museum curator and expert paleontologist there, studied Brown's newly discovered fossils.

11
00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:20.209
Osborn wrote the first scientific paper on the new dinosaur, and he also gave it its name: Tyrannosaurus rex or "tyrant lizard king."

12
00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:26.929
The Tyrannosaurus rex —or "T. rex," as it was soon nicknamed—certainly seemed to deserve its

13
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:31.873
name. The skeletons Osborn studied revealed a huge and terrifying

14
00:01:31.874 --> 00:01:36.053
creature. In adult form it was 15 meters from head to

15
00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:43.942
tail. It walked on its two back feet and stood almost seven meters high, sometimes weighing over six

16
00:01:43.943 --> 00:01:55.157
tons. With a huge skull, and about sixty long, sharp teeth, the dinosaur could tear off more than two hundred kilograms of flesh in a single

17
00:01:55.158 --> 00:02:00.904
bite! Osborn also realized that the Tyrannosaurus rex had lived during the late Cretaceous

18
00:02:00.905 --> 00:02:08.740
period. The Cretaceous period began 136 million years ago and ended 65 million years ago.

19
00:02:08.741 --> 00:02:16.742
During this time palm trees, ferns, and flowering plants grew, but grasses had yet to evolve.

20
00:02:16.743 --> 00:02:25.619
Huge flying reptiles called pterosaurs flew overhead while frogs and turtles moved along below in the land and water.

21
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:32.219
The only mammals in existence at the time were very small, similar in size and shape to mice or rats.

22
00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:39.896
In this ancient world, the giant Tyrannosaurus rex was probably truly a lizard king!

23
00:02:39.897 --> 00:02:47.502
Six years after his first discovery, Barnum Brown found another Tyrannosaurus rex at the Montana site.

24
00:02:47.503 --> 00:02:56.123
This second skeleton had more bones than the first one, so it was another exciting and useful discovery for the field of paleontology.

25
00:02:56.124 --> 00:03:01.892
Since Brown's early discoveries, more T. rex skeletons have been found.

26
00:03:01.893 --> 00:03:10.352
In 1990 paleontologist Susan Hendrickson collected a skeleton that was the biggest and most complete skeleton ever.

27
00:03:10.353 --> 00:03:19.878
It was named Sue in Hendrickson's honor, and eventually sold to a Chicago museum for lots of money—about 8.4 million dollars!

28
00:03:19.879 --> 00:03:28.019
In 2000, five more T. rex skeletons were excavated from the Montana site where Barnum Brown had discovered the first ones.

29
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:35.392
Some of these newer dinosaurs were very well-preserved, adding more information to experts' growing knowledge. As

30
00:03:35.393 --> 00:03:44.366
a clearer picture of the T. rex emerged, scientists learned that it had probably reached its full size at around age twenty, as humans do. Its

31
00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:50.971
front legs were short but quite strong, and its back legs had three toes with claws. The

32
00:03:50.972 --> 00:04:01.014
T. rex probably ate smaller plant-eating dinosaurs, maybe hunting by hiding behind trees, waiting to attack its unsuspecting prey. With

33
00:04:01.015 --> 00:04:05.759
its sharp teeth, the T. rex could easily rip into food. But

34
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.331
the dinosaur probably didn't just hunt for food. Instead

35
00:04:09.332 --> 00:04:17.057
it likely scavenged food as well, stealing it from other dinosaurs, and eating their leftovers. Bite

36
00:04:17.058 --> 00:04:25.385
marks found on the bones of several T. rex suggest that the T. rex may have even dined on other T. rex dinosaurs! Scientists

37
00:04:25.386 --> 00:04:29.101
have debated how quickly the T. rex could move. They

38
00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:35.197
think it probably moved pretty fast—about 16-32 kilometers per hour. But

39
00:04:35.198 --> 00:04:41.761
because of its huge size, the Tyrannosaurus rex was not as fast as some smaller dinosaurs. Scientists

40
00:04:41.762 --> 00:04:46.122
have also carefully examined the T. rex's forearms. The

41
00:04:46.123 --> 00:04:52.123
arms were quite short compared to its massive body, and had two fingers attached. These

42
00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:56.336
short arms might have been long enough for the dinosaur to grab prey. But they

43
00:04:56.337 --> 00:04:59.467
were definitely too short to reach its mouth. So it's

44
00:04:59.468 --> 00:05:05.119
been difficult to figure out exactly how the T. rex used these small limbs. Today, over

45
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:10.951
one hundred years after Barnum Brown’s exciting discoveries in Montana, paleontologists have

46
00:05:10.952 --> 00:05:16.491
more sophisticated tools for the excavation and study of fossils. But when

47
00:05:16.492 --> 00:05:21.445
it comes to the T. rex, they still have many more questions than answers. Perhaps future

48
00:05:21.446 --> 00:05:32.591
fossil excavations will reveal more about this amazing and massive creature— Tyrannosaurus rex , the tyrant lizard king.

